## Check Cluster Status

After this, check status of the nodes within Slurm by using the `sinfo` command.
Continue after every compute node is in an *idle* state (without asterisk). To
check one node:

```bash
sinfo -N -n ${compute_prefix}1
```

If the state is reported as *unknown*, the following might help:

```bash
scontrol update partition=normal state=idle
```

<!-- ohpc_begin -->
<!-- ohpc_comment Wait for the compute nodes to be idle in Slurm -->
<!-- ohpc_command for ((t=0; ; t++)) ; do -->
<!-- ohpc_command     [ "$(sinfo -h -r -N -t idle -o '%N' | sort -u | wc -l)" -ge "${num_computes}" ] \ -->
<!-- ohpc_command         && { echo "Slurm compute nodes: OK"; break; } -->
<!-- ohpc_command     (( t < 30 )) || { echo "Slurm compute nodes: FAILED, not idle after 300s"; sinfo; exit 1; } -->
<!-- ohpc_command     echo "Waiting for the compute nodes to be idle in Slurm..." -->
<!-- ohpc_command     sleep 10 -->
<!-- ohpc_command done -->
<!-- ohpc_end -->

In case of additional Slurm issues, ensure that the configuration file fits your
hardware and that it is identical across the nodes. Also, verify that the Slurm
user id is the same on the head node and *compute* nodes. You may also consult
[Slurm Troubleshooting Guide](https://slurm.schedmd.com/troubleshoot.html).
